window.ALFORD_WALK = {"mapLabels":{"labels":[{"id":"jacob","p":[1070,-180],"title":"Jacob Alford’s heirs","sub":"The river claim · 1831","class":"jacob"},{"id":"john","p":[-140,3710],"title":"John S. Alford","sub":"Patents · 1858","class":"john"},{"id":"seaborn","p":[770,5510],"title":"Seaborn L. Alford","sub":"Homestead · 1881","class":"seaborn"},{"id":"river","p":[3850,2200],"title":"Bogue Chitto","sub":"","class":"river-label"},{"id":"riverSouth","p":[2570,4850],"title":"Bogue Chitto","sub":"","class":"river-label"}],"records":["landscape","survey","patents","homestead"],"body":"Historical land reconstructed on modern river geography.","html":"<p>Historical land reconstructed on modern river geography.<\/p>"},"scenes":[{"id":"beginning","year":"1768–2008","title":"The Alford family","visual":"map","records":["memorial","parentage","escoParentage","maryObit","will"],"people":["christine"],"part":0,"hero":true,"body":"Christine Alford Knight grew up in a farming family near Mount Hermon, Louisiana. She became a nurse, moved to Baton Rouge and spent thirty-three years in public health.\n\nBefore her, three generations of the family had lived near the Bogue Chitto River.\n\nWe begin with a possible earlier branch in North Carolina. That connection is still uncertain. The family line becomes clearer in Louisiana with Christine’s great-grandparents, John and Margaret.","html":"<p>Christine Alford Knight grew up in a farming family near Mount Hermon, Louisiana. She became a nurse, moved to Baton Rouge and spent thirty-three years in public health.<\/p>\n<p>Before her, three generations of the family had lived near the Bogue Chitto River.<\/p>\n<p>We begin with a possible earlier branch in North Carolina. That connection is still uncertain. The family line becomes clearer in Louisiana with Christine’s great-grandparents, John and Margaret.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"north-carolina","year":"1768","title":"Who was Julius Alford?","visual":"regional","records":["will"],"people":[],"part":1,"place":"North Carolina","region":"NC","regionLabel":"Modern Franklin County area · plantation unlocated","refs":"C-139; C-140; S-1711","body":"Julius Alford lived beside the Tar River in colonial North Carolina. He owned a plantation, was married to Lucy, and named seven children in his will.\n\nWe start with Julius because his son Jacob may have been the father of Christine’s great-grandfather, John, in Louisiana. That connection is still an open question.","html":"<p>Julius Alford lived beside the Tar River in colonial North Carolina. He owned a plantation, was married to Lucy, and named seven children in his will.<\/p>\n<p>We start with Julius because his son Jacob may have been the father of Christine’s great-grandfather, John, in Louisiana. That connection is still an open question.<\/p>"},{"id":"will","year":"1768","title":"Julius provides for his family","visual":"image","records":["will"],"people":[],"image":"will","quote":"the Land & Plantation whereon I Now Live Lying on both Sides [Tar] River","quoteLabel":"Excerpt · Julius Alford’s will","place":"North Carolina","refs":"S-1130; S-1711","body":"In 1768, Julius set out how his family should be provided for after his death. His will allowed Lucy to use the remaining property during her lifetime or widowhood, and directed income from the estate toward maintaining and schooling the children.\n\nJacob was to receive the home plantation on both sides of the Tar River, along with livestock and a breeding mare.","html":"<p>In 1768, Julius set out how his family should be provided for after his death. His will allowed Lucy to use the remaining property during her lifetime or widowhood, and directed income from the estate toward maintaining and schooling the children.<\/p>\n<p>Jacob was to receive the home plantation on both sides of the Tar River, along with livestock and a breeding mare.<\/p>"},{"id":"deed","year":"1789","title":"Julius’s son Jacob","visual":"image","records":["deed"],"people":[],"image":"deed","place":"Franklin County, North Carolina","roi":[2260,235,4160,750],"detailImage":true,"body":"In 1789, Jacob sold Simon Williams his right to a share of his father’s estate that he was to receive after his mother’s death. The agreement identifies him as “the son of Julius Alford deceased.”","html":"<p>In 1789, Jacob sold Simon Williams his right to a share of his father’s estate that he was to receive after his mother’s death. The agreement identifies him as “the son of Julius Alford deceased.”<\/p>"},{"id":"south-carolina","year":"Before 1807","title":"A family moves to Louisiana","visual":"regional","records":["twin","census1880"],"people":[],"place":"South Carolina","region":"SC","regionLabel":"South Carolina · no locality named in the account","body":"John Alford’s father was named Jacob. According to a later obituary of John’s twin brother, Jacob came from South Carolina shortly before the boys were born in 1807.\n\nThe account says their father died while they were still children, leaving their mother a widow. It does not give her name.","html":"<p>John Alford’s father was named Jacob. According to a later obituary of John’s twin brother, Jacob came from South Carolina shortly before the boys were born in 1807.<\/p>\n<p>The account says their father died while they were still children, leaving their mother a widow. It does not give her name.<\/p>"},{"id":"twins","year":"1807","title":"John and his twin brother","visual":"twins","records":["twin","farm"],"people":["john"],"place":"Near modern Osyka","cards":[["TWIN · 1807","John Seaborn","Christine’s great-grandfather"],["TWIN · 1807","Seaborn John","John’s brother · Methodist preacher"]],"graphicNote":"Two brothers with similar names. The younger Seaborn L. belongs to the next generation.","body":"John Seaborn Alford and Seaborn John Alford were twins. Their later family account gives their birth as 11 October 1807, in Louisiana near present-day Osyka, Mississippi.\n\nWe follow John, Christine’s great-grandfather. He became a farmer in Washington Parish. His brother became a Methodist preacher.","html":"<p>John Seaborn Alford and Seaborn John Alford were twins. Their later family account gives their birth as 11 October 1807, in Louisiana near present-day Osyka, Mississippi.<\/p>\n<p>We follow John, Christine’s great-grandfather. He became a farmer in Washington Parish. His brother became a Methodist preacher.<\/p>"},{"id":"river","year":"1831","title":"Jacob’s land beside the Bogue Chitto","visual":"claim","records":["survey","patents","homestead"],"people":[],"body":"A Jacob Alford had claimed land beside the Bogue Chitto River by 1807. By 1831 he was dead, and the land was surveyed for his heirs.\n\nHe may have been the twins’ father. His claim lay in the same township where John and, later, John’s son Seaborn acquired land.","html":"<p>A Jacob Alford had claimed land beside the Bogue Chitto River by 1807. By 1831 he was dead, and the land was surveyed for his heirs.<\/p>\n<p>He may have been the twins’ father. His claim lay in the same township where John and, later, John’s son Seaborn acquired land.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"survey","year":"1831","title":"The river boundary","visual":"image","records":["survey"],"people":[],"image":"survey","body":"The claim covered about 636 acres—almost a square mile. In the surveyor’s drawing, the Bogue Chitto River forms its eastern edge.\n\nThis is the original survey. The map behind it places the claim in the surrounding landscape.","html":"<p>The claim covered about 636 acres—almost a square mile. In the surveyor’s drawing, the Bogue Chitto River forms its eastern edge.<\/p>\n<p>This is the original survey. The map behind it places the claim in the surrounding landscape.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"heirs","year":"1831","title":"Who inherited Jacob’s land?","visual":"image","records":["survey","patents","parentage"],"people":[],"image":"survey","roi":[720,475,1890,1140],"quiet":true,"detailImage":true,"body":"The drawing is labeled “Heirs Jacob Alford.” It records the land for his heirs but does not name them, so it cannot tell us whether John was among them.\n\nJohn’s own land records take us farther south in the same township. From here, we can follow his family through to Christine with much greater confidence.","html":"<p>The drawing is labeled “Heirs Jacob Alford.” It records the land for his heirs but does not name them, so it cannot tell us whether John was among them.<\/p>\n<p>John’s own land records take us farther south in the same township. From here, we can follow his family through to Christine with much greater confidence.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"marriage","year":"1833","title":"John and Margaret start a family","visual":"family","records":["margaret","parentage"],"people":["john","margaret"],"family":["john","margaret","seaborn"],"part":2,"familyCaption":"Christine’s great-grandparents","childLabel":"THEIR SON · BORN 1844","body":"John married Margaret in 1833. The dates in her church obituary make him about twenty-six and her about fourteen. Their marriage lasted more than fifty years.\n\nThey raised a large family in Washington Parish. We follow their son Seaborn, who would become Christine’s grandfather.","html":"<p>John married Margaret in 1833. The dates in her church obituary make him about twenty-six and her about fourteen. Their marriage lasted more than fifty years.<\/p>\n<p>They raised a large family in Washington Parish. We follow their son Seaborn, who would become Christine’s grandfather.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"son","year":"1844","title":"Their son, Seaborn","visual":"family","records":["parentage","seabornObit"],"people":["seaborn"],"family":["john","margaret","seaborn"],"child":true,"familyCaption":"Christine’s great-grandparents","childLabel":"THEIR SON · BORN 1844","body":"Seaborn L. Alford was born in May 1844 and grew up in John and Margaret’s household.\n\nHe belongs to the next generation: John’s son, rather than John’s twin brother. He would farm nearby, serve in the Civil War and become the father of Esco, Christine’s father.","html":"<p>Seaborn L. Alford was born in May 1844 and grew up in John and Margaret’s household.<\/p>\n<p>He belongs to the next generation: John’s son, rather than John’s twin brother. He would farm nearby, serve in the Civil War and become the father of Esco, Christine’s father.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"farm","year":"1850","title":"John’s farm","visual":"farm","records":["farm"],"people":[],"graphicLabels":["40 improved acres","600 unimproved acres","One square = 10 acres · reported acreage, not a parcel map"],"body":"John’s farm produced corn and oats. The 1850 farm return associated with him also lists four horses.\n\nMost of its reported acreage was classed as unimproved: six hundred acres, compared with forty acres of improved land.","html":"<p>John’s farm produced corn and oats. The 1850 farm return associated with him also lists four horses.<\/p>\n<p>Most of its reported acreage was classed as unimproved: six hundred acres, compared with forty acres of improved land.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"township","year":"1851","title":"Locating the family’s land","visual":"image","records":["plat","field"],"people":[],"image":"plat","body":"This 1851 map divides the township into older land claims and smaller numbered lots. John and Seaborn later acquired several of those lots.\n\nMatching the lot numbers lets us place their land on the map. Their houses have not been located, but we can see how close the two generations’ properties were.","html":"<p>This 1851 map divides the township into older land claims and smaller numbered lots. John and Seaborn later acquired several of those lots.<\/p>\n<p>Matching the lot numbers lets us place their land on the map. Their houses have not been located, but we can see how close the two generations’ properties were.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"field","year":"1851","title":"A field on John’s later land","visual":"image","records":["field"],"people":[],"image":"plat","roi":[115,625,565,935],"quiet":true,"detailImage":true,"body":"The surveyor marked a FIELD that extended into a lot John would acquire in 1858. The ground was already being worked seven years before his land grant.\n\nWe do not know who was farming it in 1851. John was living in the parish by then, but the map does not name the farmer.","html":"<p>The surveyor marked a FIELD that extended into a lot John would acquire in 1858. The ground was already being worked seven years before his land grant.<\/p>\n<p>We do not know who was farming it in 1851. John was living in the parish by then, but the map does not name the farmer.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"john-land","year":"1858","title":"John acquires land","visual":"john","records":["patents","field","landscape"],"people":["john"],"body":"In 1858, John received federal title to several lots totaling nearly ninety-four acres. The green areas show these separate pieces of land.\n\nThe family cemetery was later recorded inside one of the lots. This was a family burial ground, separate from the cemetery at Bogue Chitto church.","html":"<p>In 1858, John received federal title to several lots totaling nearly ninety-four acres. The green areas show these separate pieces of land.<\/p>\n<p>The family cemetery was later recorded inside one of the lots. This was a family burial ground, separate from the cemetery at Bogue Chitto church.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"war","year":"1862","title":"Seaborn goes to war","visual":"image","records":["muster","parole","portHudsonWorks"],"people":["seaborn"],"image":"portHudsonWorks","place":"Camp Moore · Port Hudson","roi":[420,315,2750,2690],"body":"In May 1862, John and Margaret’s son Seaborn enlisted in the Confederate army, just before his eighteenth birthday. He joined at Camp Moore and served in a unit that became the 3rd Louisiana Cavalry.\n\nBy the following summer, he was at Port Hudson, where Union forces were besieging the Confederate position.","html":"<p>In May 1862, John and Margaret’s son Seaborn enlisted in the Confederate army, just before his eighteenth birthday. He joined at Camp Moore and served in a unit that became the 3rd Louisiana Cavalry.<\/p>\n<p>By the following summer, he was at Port Hudson, where Union forces were besieging the Confederate position.<\/p>"},{"id":"port-hudson","year":"1863","title":"Captured at Port Hudson","visual":"image","records":["portHudsonBattery","parole"],"people":[],"image":"portHudsonBattery","place":"Port Hudson, Louisiana","roi":[240,340,2650,2680],"quiet":true,"body":"Seaborn was captured at Port Hudson on 9 July 1863 and released on parole a few days later.\n\nThe photograph shows Union artillery during the siege—the forces opposing him. It gives a view of the war he experienced, although it does not show Seaborn or his unit.","html":"<p>Seaborn was captured at Port Hudson on 9 July 1863 and released on parole a few days later.<\/p>\n<p>The photograph shows Union artillery during the siege—the forces opposing him. It gives a view of the war he experienced, although it does not show Seaborn or his unit.<\/p>"},{"id":"laura","year":"1866","title":"Seaborn marries Laura","visual":"family","records":["seabornObit","escoParentage","laura"],"people":["seaborn","laura"],"family":["seaborn","laura","esco"],"familyCaption":"Christine’s grandparents","body":"After the war, Seaborn married Laura Ellzey on Christmas Eve, 1866. Both were about twenty-two.\n\nThey had ten children, according to his later obituary. One was Esco, the son through whom this story reaches Christine. Laura was probably the daughter of John and Elizabeth Ellzey.","html":"<p>After the war, Seaborn married Laura Ellzey on Christmas Eve, 1866. Both were about twenty-two.<\/p>\n<p>They had ten children, according to his later obituary. One was Esco, the son through whom this story reaches Christine. Laura was probably the daughter of John and Elizabeth Ellzey.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"esco","year":"1878","title":"Esco, Christine’s father","visual":"image","records":["escoDraft","escoParentage","escoObit"],"people":["esco"],"image":"escoDraft","body":"Esco Seaborn Alford was born on 21 May 1878. In 1880, he appears as a two-year-old in Seaborn and Laura’s household.\n\nHe would follow his father into farming. The card shown here was completed by the adult Esco in 1918; it preserves the birth date he gave himself.","html":"<p>Esco Seaborn Alford was born on 21 May 1878. In 1880, he appears as a two-year-old in Seaborn and Laura’s household.<\/p>\n<p>He would follow his father into farming. The card shown here was completed by the adult Esco in 1918; it preserves the birth date he gave himself.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"homestead","year":"1881","title":"A farm near his father’s","visual":"seaborn","records":["homestead","patents","landscape"],"people":["seaborn"],"body":"In 1881, Seaborn received title to a homestead of about 135 acres. Its three lots lay south of the land his father, John, had acquired.\n\nThe two generations had separate properties within the same township. The map shows that proximity; it does not mark their houses or imply that one farm passed from father to son.","html":"<p>In 1881, Seaborn received title to a homestead of about 135 acres. Its three lots lay south of the land his father, John, had acquired.<\/p>\n<p>The two generations had separate properties within the same township. The map shows that proximity; it does not mark their houses or imply that one farm passed from father to son.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"margaret","year":"1885","title":"Margaret’s life in the churches","visual":"image","records":["margaret"],"people":["margaret"],"image":"margaret","body":"Margaret had been married to John for more than fifty years when she died in September 1885. Her church remembered a large family: a husband, fourteen surviving children and forty-three grandchildren.\n\nIt also recorded her religious life. She joined the Methodists in 1853, was baptized at Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866, and later joined Bogue Chitto church.","html":"<p>Margaret had been married to John for more than fifty years when she died in September 1885. Her church remembered a large family: a husband, fourteen surviving children and forty-three grandchildren.<\/p>\n<p>It also recorded her religious life. She joined the Methodists in 1853, was baptized at Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866, and later joined Bogue Chitto church.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"mary-lou","year":"1899","title":"Esco and Mary Lou","visual":"family","records":["escoMarriage","maryObit","census1900"],"people":["esco","mary-lou"],"family":["esco","mary-lou","christine"],"part":3,"familyCaption":"Christine’s parents","childLabel":"THEIR DAUGHTER · ABOUT 1921","body":"Esco married Mary Lou Fussell in December 1899. She was also known as Lou and was the daughter of Henry T. Fussell. They would become Christine’s parents.\n\nBy the following year, the newly married couple had their own farm household near Esco’s family.","html":"<p>Esco married Mary Lou Fussell in December 1899. She was also known as Lou and was the daughter of Henry T. Fussell. They would become Christine’s parents.<\/p>\n<p>By the following year, the newly married couple had their own farm household near Esco’s family.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"households","year":"1900","title":"A household near his father’s","visual":"households","records":["census1900"],"people":["esco","mary-lou"],"cards":[["THE OLDER GENERATION","Seaborn & Laura","Esco’s parents"],["A SEPARATE HOUSEHOLD","Esco & Mary Lou","Married in 1899"]],"graphicNote":"Related households in the census sequence. This diagram does not locate or space their homes.","body":"In 1900, Esco and Mary Lou were living on a farm he owned. His father and a brother appear nearby in the census taker’s list of households.\n\nThat suggests they were neighbours as well as relatives. It does not tell us how far apart their houses stood.","html":"<p>In 1900, Esco and Mary Lou were living on a farm he owned. His father and a brother appear nearby in the census taker’s list of households.<\/p>\n<p>That suggests they were neighbours as well as relatives. It does not tell us how far apart their houses stood.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"laura-death","year":"1903","title":"The death of Esco’s mother","visual":"image","records":["laura","seabornObit","escoDraft","census1900"],"people":["laura"],"image":"laura","body":"Laura died in March 1903, after more than thirty-six years of marriage to Seaborn. Esco was twenty-four and had already established a household with Mary Lou.\n\nHer marker stands in the family cemetery. The photograph is worn and difficult to read; her dates are preserved in an earlier cemetery transcription.","html":"<p>Laura died in March 1903, after more than thirty-six years of marriage to Seaborn. Esco was twenty-four and had already established a household with Mary Lou.<\/p>\n<p>Her marker stands in the family cemetery. The photograph is worn and difficult to read; her dates are preserved in an earlier cemetery transcription.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"candacy","year":"1909","title":"Seaborn’s second family","visual":"timeline","records":["seabornObit","candacyObit"],"people":["seaborn"],"milestones":[["1866","Marriage to Laura","Ten children"],["1909","Marriage to Candacy","Three children"]],"body":"Six years after Laura’s death, Seaborn married Candacy Bankston. He was sixty-five. They had three children: Jacob, Frankie and Agnes.\n\nThese were Esco’s younger half-siblings. He was already an adult with a household of his own when this second family began.","html":"<p>Six years after Laura’s death, Seaborn married Candacy Bankston. He was sixty-five. They had three children: Jacob, Frankie and Agnes.<\/p>\n<p>These were Esco’s younger half-siblings. He was already an adult with a household of his own when this second family began.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"seaborn-death","year":"1919","title":"A life spent near home","visual":"all-land","records":["seabornObit","memorial","landscape"],"people":["seaborn"],"body":"Seaborn died at home in November 1919, aged seventy-five. His obituary placed the house three miles south of Warnerton and said he died “in sight of the place of his birth.”\n\nHe had served in a war and raised children from two marriages, yet his home was still close to where he had been born. Christine, his granddaughter, would be born a little over a year after his death.","html":"<p>Seaborn died at home in November 1919, aged seventy-five. His obituary placed the house three miles south of Warnerton and said he died “in sight of the place of his birth.”<\/p>\n<p>He had served in a war and raised children from two marriages, yet his home was still close to where he had been born. Christine, his granddaughter, would be born a little over a year after his death.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"christine","year":"1921","title":"Christine is born","visual":"family","records":["memorial","census1930"],"people":["christine"],"family":["esco","mary-lou","christine"],"child":true,"place":"Mount Hermon, Louisiana","familyCaption":"Christine’s parents","childLabel":"THEIR DAUGHTER · ABOUT 1921","body":"Christine was born to Esco and Mary Lou around the beginning of 1921. Her later obituary gives New Year’s Day and a farm near Mount Hermon as her birthplace.\n\nShe was Seaborn and Laura’s granddaughter and John and Margaret’s great-granddaughter. This was the farming family she grew up in.","html":"<p>Christine was born to Esco and Mary Lou around the beginning of 1921. Her later obituary gives New Year’s Day and a farm near Mount Hermon as her birthplace.<\/p>\n<p>She was Seaborn and Laura’s granddaughter and John and Margaret’s great-granddaughter. This was the farming family she grew up in.<\/p>"},{"id":"census","year":"1930","title":"Christine at nine","visual":"image","records":["census1930","maryObit"],"people":["christine"],"image":"census1930","roi":[280,230,1560,747],"detailImage":true,"body":"In 1930, nine-year-old Christine was living with her parents and her sixteen-year-old brother, Tolbert. Her father was a farmer, and she was attending school.\n\nThe family appears together on these four lines: Esco, Lou, Tolbert and Christine.","html":"<p>In 1930, nine-year-old Christine was living with her parents and her sixteen-year-old brother, Tolbert. Her father was a farmer, and she was attending school.<\/p>\n<p>The family appears together on these four lines: Esco, Lou, Tolbert and Christine.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"touro","year":"1941","title":"Christine becomes a nurse","visual":"image","records":["nursing","touroPhoto"],"people":["christine"],"image":"touroPhoto","part":4,"place":"New Orleans, Louisiana","body":"By age twenty, Christine had trained as a nurse in New Orleans. She graduated from Touro Infirmary’s nursing school in August 1941.\n\nHer education took her from the Mount Hermon farming community to a city hospital. This earlier photograph shows Touro around 1910, before her time there.","html":"<p>By age twenty, Christine had trained as a nurse in New Orleans. She graduated from Touro Infirmary’s nursing school in August 1941.<\/p>\n<p>Her education took her from the Mount Hermon farming community to a city hospital. This earlier photograph shows Touro around 1910, before her time there.<\/p>"},{"id":"graduation","year":"1941","title":"Her father brings her home","visual":"image","records":["nursing"],"people":["esco","christine"],"image":"nursing","place":"New Orleans · Mount Hermon","roi":[1045,1490,1410,1730],"detailImage":true,"body":"After Christine’s graduation, Esco drove to New Orleans to bring her home. The hometown paper reported a three-week vacation before she returned to Touro to work as a nurse.\n\nHer working life was beginning in the city, while her family remained in the Mount Hermon community.","html":"<p>After Christine’s graduation, Esco drove to New Orleans to bring her home. The hometown paper reported a three-week vacation before she returned to Touro to work as a nurse.<\/p>\n<p>Her working life was beginning in the city, while her family remained in the Mount Hermon community.<\/p>"},{"id":"baton-rouge","year":"1942–1943","title":"Public-health work in Baton Rouge","visual":"cities","records":["directory","nursing"],"people":["christine"],"place":"Baton Rouge, Louisiana","locations":[{"name":"MOUNT HERMON","year":"Childhood","role":"Family community","coordinates":[-90.2959307,30.9601859]},{"name":"NEW ORLEANS","year":"1941","role":"Touro nursing school","coordinates":[-90.092608,29.924923]},{"name":"BATON ROUGE","year":"1942–1943","role":"Parish Health Unit","coordinates":[-91.175538,30.418439]}],"graphicNote":"Places in Christine’s life. These points mark communities and institutions; the lines do not trace known travel routes.","body":"By 1942, Christine was working for the Parish Health Unit in Baton Rouge. The city directory lists her as a nurse there in both 1942 and 1943.\n\nAfter training in a hospital, she had moved into public-health work. Mount Hermon, New Orleans and Baton Rouge mark the places where she grew up, trained and worked.","html":"<p>By 1942, Christine was working for the Parish Health Unit in Baton Rouge. The city directory lists her as a nurse there in both 1942 and 1943.<\/p>\n<p>After training in a hospital, she had moved into public-health work. Mount Hermon, New Orleans and Baton Rouge mark the places where she grew up, trained and worked.<\/p>"},{"id":"peabody","year":"1944","title":"More training in public health","visual":"timeline","records":["memorial","nursing","directory"],"people":["christine"],"milestones":[["1941","Touro Infirmary","Nursing school"],["1942–1943","Parish Health Unit","Nurse in Baton Rouge"],["1944","Peabody College","Further qualification · reported in her obituary"]],"place":"Education & work","body":"Christine continued her education while building a nursing career. Her later obituary reports a public-health qualification from Peabody College in 1944.\n\nShe was already working in public health by then. This further training fits the career she would pursue for the next three decades.","html":"<p>Christine continued her education while building a nursing career. Her later obituary reports a public-health qualification from Peabody College in 1944.<\/p>\n<p>She was already working in public health by then. This further training fits the career she would pursue for the next three decades.<\/p>"},{"id":"christine-marriage","year":"1946","title":"Christine marries Charles Knight","visual":"family","records":["marriage","census1950"],"people":["christine","charles"],"family":["christine","charles"],"place":"Bienville Parish, Louisiana","body":"Christine married Charles Binford Knight in Bienville Parish on 1 February 1946. She was about twenty-five.\n\nThe couple later made their home in Baton Rouge, where she continued nursing and he worked at a funeral home. She would be known as Christine Alford Knight.","html":"<p>Christine married Charles Binford Knight in Bienville Parish on 1 February 1946. She was about twenty-five.<\/p>\n<p>The couple later made their home in Baton Rouge, where she continued nursing and he worked at a funeral home. She would be known as Christine Alford Knight.<\/p>"},{"id":"mary-lou-death","year":"1946","title":"Marriage and loss in the same week","visual":"image","records":["maryObit","marriage"],"people":["mary-lou"],"image":"maryObit","place":"Mount Hermon, Louisiana","body":"Three days after Christine’s marriage date, her mother, Mary Lou, died at home near Mount Hermon. It was 4 February 1946.\n\nThe funeral was to be held at Bogue Chitto church. The death notice names her daughter by her new married name, “Mrs. Christine Knight.”","html":"<p>Three days after Christine’s marriage date, her mother, Mary Lou, died at home near Mount Hermon. It was 4 February 1946.<\/p>\n<p>The funeral was to be held at Bogue Chitto church. The death notice names her daughter by her new married name, “Mrs. Christine Knight.”<\/p>"},{"id":"lynnie","year":"1946–1947","title":"Esco remarries","visual":"timeline","records":["escoMarriage"],"people":["esco"],"milestones":[["4 February 1946","Mary Lou dies","Christine’s mother"],["30 April 1946","Esco marries Lynnie","His second wife"],["1947","Lynnie dies","Widowed again"]],"place":"Louisiana","body":"After Mary Lou’s death, Esco married Lynnie Schilling in April 1946. Lynnie died the following year. Esco had lost two wives in less than two years.","html":"<p>After Mary Lou’s death, Esco married Lynnie Schilling in April 1946. Lynnie died the following year. Esco had lost two wives in less than two years.<\/p>"},{"id":"adult-household","year":"1950","title":"Two working lives in Baton Rouge","visual":"adult-census","records":["census1950","directory"],"people":["christine","charles"],"place":"Baton Rouge, Louisiana","graphicLabel":"CHRISTINE & CHARLES · 1950","adults":[["Christine A. Knight","Registered nurse · parish health","Government employment · 40 hours"],["Charles B. Knight","Assistant manager · funeral home","Baton Rouge, Louisiana"]],"graphicNote":"Selected adult entries from the 1950 census.","body":"By 1950, Christine and Charles were living in Baton Rouge. She worked a forty-hour week as a parish health nurse; he was an assistant manager at a funeral home.\n\nChristine had continued in her profession after marriage. Her public-health career was now well established.","html":"<p>By 1950, Christine and Charles were living in Baton Rouge. She worked a forty-hour week as a parish health nurse; he was an assistant manager at a funeral home.<\/p>\n<p>Christine had continued in her profession after marriage. Her public-health career was now well established.<\/p>"},{"id":"florence","year":"1951","title":"Esco marries Florence Bennett","visual":"timeline","records":["escoMarriage","escoObit"],"people":["esco"],"quiet":true,"milestones":[["1899","Mary Lou Fussell","Died in 1946"],["1946","Lynnie Schilling","Died in 1947"],["1951","Florence Bennett","Later life little known"]],"place":"Louisiana","body":"In May 1951, Esco married Florence Bennett. We know little about their life together beyond this marriage record.","html":"<p>In May 1951, Esco married Florence Bennett. We know little about their life together beyond this marriage record.<\/p>"},{"id":"esco-death","year":"1958","title":"Esco’s place in the community","visual":"image","records":["escoObit","escoPortrait"],"people":["esco"],"image":"escoPortrait","place":"Tylertown · Bogue Chitto","body":"Esco was a farmer and dairyman. He was also a church deacon and served as a parish jury commissioner.\n\nHe died after a stroke in April 1958 and was buried at Bogue Chitto Baptist Church. Christine, living in Baton Rouge, was among his surviving children.","html":"<p>Esco was a farmer and dairyman. He was also a church deacon and served as a parish jury commissioner.<\/p>\n<p>He died after a stroke in April 1958 and was buried at Bogue Chitto Baptist Church. Christine, living in Baton Rouge, was among his surviving children.<\/p>"},{"id":"retirement","year":"1975","title":"Thirty-three years in public health","visual":"timeline","records":["memorial","directory","census1950"],"people":["christine"],"part":5,"milestones":[["1942–1943","Parish Health Unit","Nurse in Baton Rouge"],["1950","Registered nurse","Full-time work in parish health"],["1975","Retirement","After 33 years, according to her obituary"]],"place":"Louisiana","body":"Christine retired in 1975 after thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department, according to her later obituary.\n\nHer work included coordinating clinics for children with disabilities across several parishes.","html":"<p>Christine retired in 1975 after thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department, according to her later obituary.<\/p>\n<p>Her work included coordinating clinics for children with disabilities across several parishes.<\/p>"},{"id":"christine-death","year":"2008","title":"Christine’s life, and the generations before her","visual":"lineage","records":["memorial","parentage","escoParentage","maryObit","farm","homestead","escoObit","nursing","directory"],"people":["christine"],"place":"Christine’s family","graphicNote":"John and Margaret’s son Seaborn married Laura. Seaborn and Laura’s son Esco married Mary Lou. Esco and Mary Lou’s daughter was Christine. Select a name for the supporting records.","body":"Christine died in 2008. She had spent thirty-three years in public health, been married to Charles for forty-four years, and served with the Trinity Episcopal Church Altar Guild. Charles died before her.\n\nHer father Esco, grandfather Seaborn and great-grandfather John had farmed in the Bogue Chitto area. Her own working life took her to New Orleans and Baton Rouge.","html":"<p>Christine died in 2008. She had spent thirty-three years in public health, been married to Charles for forty-four years, and served with the Trinity Episcopal Church Altar Guild. Charles died before her.<\/p>\n<p>Her father Esco, grandfather Seaborn and great-grandfather John had farmed in the Bogue Chitto area. Her own working life took her to New Orleans and Baton Rouge.<\/p>"}],"people":{"margaret":{"id":"margaret","name":"Margaret Brumfield Alford","dates":"1819–1885","relationship":"Christine’s great-grandmother","archiveKey":"margaret-brumfield","refs":"S-1572","records":["margaret","parentage"],"body":"Margaret was John Alford’s wife and Christine’s great-grandmother. Her church obituary says they married in 1833, when she was about fourteen. At her death in 1885, it counted a husband, fourteen surviving children and forty-three grandchildren.\n\nShe joined the Methodists in 1853, was baptized into Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866 and later joined Bogue Chitto church. These changes in membership are among the few details we have of her life beyond her family.\n\nHer maiden name, Brumfield, comes from later family compilations; the church obituary calls her Margaret Alford. Census and family records support John and Margaret as the parents of Seaborn, Christine’s grandfather.","html":"<p>Margaret was John Alford’s wife and Christine’s great-grandmother. Her church obituary says they married in 1833, when she was about fourteen. At her death in 1885, it counted a husband, fourteen surviving children and forty-three grandchildren.<\/p>\n<p>She joined the Methodists in 1853, was baptized into Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866 and later joined Bogue Chitto church. These changes in membership are among the few details we have of her life beyond her family.<\/p>\n<p>Her maiden name, Brumfield, comes from later family compilations; the church obituary calls her Margaret Alford. Census and family records support John and Margaret as the parents of Seaborn, Christine’s grandfather.<\/p>"},"mary-lou":{"id":"mary-lou","name":"Mary Lou Fussell Alford","dates":"1883 or 1884–1946","relationship":"Christine’s mother","archiveKey":"mary-louisa-fussell","records":["escoMarriage","maryObit","census1930","census1900","marriage"],"body":"Mary Lou Fussell Alford, also called Lou, was Christine’s mother. She married Esco in December 1899, and they made their home in the farming community near Mount Hermon. In 1930, their household included sixteen-year-old Tolbert and nine-year-old Christine.\n\nHer father was Henry T. Fussell. Eliza or Liza Hobbs is her likely mother, although the death indexes disagree on the mother’s name. Her own birth year is also uncertain: records give 1883 and 1884.\n\nShe died at home on 4 February 1946, three days after Christine’s marriage date. Her obituary names her daughter as “Mrs. Christine Knight.”","html":"<p>Mary Lou Fussell Alford, also called Lou, was Christine’s mother. She married Esco in December 1899, and they made their home in the farming community near Mount Hermon. In 1930, their household included sixteen-year-old Tolbert and nine-year-old Christine.<\/p>\n<p>Her father was Henry T. Fussell. Eliza or Liza Hobbs is her likely mother, although the death indexes disagree on the mother’s name. Her own birth year is also uncertain: records give 1883 and 1884.<\/p>\n<p>She died at home on 4 February 1946, three days after Christine’s marriage date. Her obituary names her daughter as “Mrs. Christine Knight.”<\/p>"},"esco":{"id":"esco","name":"Esco Seaborn Alford","dates":"1878–1958","relationship":"Christine’s father","archiveKey":"P-045","records":["escoDraft","escoParentage","escoMarriage","escoObit","escoPortrait","census1900","nursing"],"body":"Esco was Christine’s father, a farmer and dairyman near Mount Hermon. He was the son of Seaborn and Laura Alford. He also served his community as a church deacon and parish jury commissioner.\n\nHe married Mary Lou Fussell in 1899. By 1900 they had their own farm household, near other members of his family. Their daughter Christine later trained as a nurse in New Orleans.\n\nAfter Mary Lou died in 1946, he married Lynnie Schilling, who died in 1947, and then Florence Bennett in 1951. He died after a stroke in April 1958 and was buried at Bogue Chitto Baptist Church.","html":"<p>Esco was Christine’s father, a farmer and dairyman near Mount Hermon. He was the son of Seaborn and Laura Alford. He also served his community as a church deacon and parish jury commissioner.<\/p>\n<p>He married Mary Lou Fussell in 1899. By 1900 they had their own farm household, near other members of his family. Their daughter Christine later trained as a nurse in New Orleans.<\/p>\n<p>After Mary Lou died in 1946, he married Lynnie Schilling, who died in 1947, and then Florence Bennett in 1951. He died after a stroke in April 1958 and was buried at Bogue Chitto Baptist Church.<\/p>"},"christine":{"id":"christine","name":"Christine Alford Knight","dates":"About 1921–2008","relationship":"The descendant followed in this story","archiveKey":"P-044","records":["census1930","nursing","directory","marriage","census1950","memorial"],"body":"Christine grew up on a farm near Mount Hermon, the daughter of Esco and Mary Lou Alford. She graduated from Touro Infirmary’s nursing school in 1941 and was working in public health in Baton Rouge by 1942.\n\nShe married Charles Binford Knight in 1946 and continued nursing. Her work included coordinating clinics for children with disabilities across several parishes. She retired in 1975 after thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department, according to her obituary.\n\nShe died in 2008, having outlived Charles. Her later obituary gives her birth as 1 January 1921; an original birth record has not been found in this research. Accounts disagree on her exact death date.","html":"<p>Christine grew up on a farm near Mount Hermon, the daughter of Esco and Mary Lou Alford. She graduated from Touro Infirmary’s nursing school in 1941 and was working in public health in Baton Rouge by 1942.<\/p>\n<p>She married Charles Binford Knight in 1946 and continued nursing. Her work included coordinating clinics for children with disabilities across several parishes. She retired in 1975 after thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department, according to her obituary.<\/p>\n<p>She died in 2008, having outlived Charles. Her later obituary gives her birth as 1 January 1921; an original birth record has not been found in this research. Accounts disagree on her exact death date.<\/p>"},"laura":{"id":"laura","name":"Laura A. Ellzey Alford","dates":"1844–1903, reported","relationship":"Christine’s grandmother","archiveKey":"P-052","records":["laura","seabornObit","escoParentage"],"body":"Laura was Seaborn Alford’s first wife, Esco’s mother and Christine’s grandmother. She and Seaborn married on Christmas Eve, 1866, and had ten children, according to his later obituary.\n\nShe was probably the daughter of John and Elizabeth Ellzey. Her surname appears in several spellings; Seaborn’s obituary calls her Laura A. Ellzy.\n\nLaura died in 1903, after more than thirty-six years of marriage. Her gravestone is shown in the story, but its dates are difficult to read. The dates used here come from an earlier cemetery transcription, with her birth date also supported by a family Bible abstract.","html":"<p>Laura was Seaborn Alford’s first wife, Esco’s mother and Christine’s grandmother. She and Seaborn married on Christmas Eve, 1866, and had ten children, according to his later obituary.<\/p>\n<p>She was probably the daughter of John and Elizabeth Ellzey. Her surname appears in several spellings; Seaborn’s obituary calls her Laura A. Ellzy.<\/p>\n<p>Laura died in 1903, after more than thirty-six years of marriage. Her gravestone is shown in the story, but its dates are difficult to read. The dates used here come from an earlier cemetery transcription, with her birth date also supported by a family Bible abstract.<\/p>"},"charles":{"id":"charles","name":"Charles Binford Knight","dates":"Married 1946","relationship":"Christine’s husband","archiveKey":"P-018","records":["marriage","census1950","memorial"],"body":"Charles Binford Knight was Christine’s husband. They married in Bienville Parish on 1 February 1946 and later lived in Baton Rouge.\n\nIn 1950, he was an assistant manager at a funeral home while Christine worked as a parish health nurse. Her obituary describes a forty-four-year marriage and says that he died before her.","html":"<p>Charles Binford Knight was Christine’s husband. They married in Bienville Parish on 1 February 1946 and later lived in Baton Rouge.<\/p>\n<p>In 1950, he was an assistant manager at a funeral home while Christine worked as a parish health nurse. Her obituary describes a forty-four-year marriage and says that he died before her.<\/p>"},"seaborn":{"id":"seaborn","name":"Seaborn L. Alford","dates":"1844–1919","relationship":"Christine’s grandfather","archiveKey":"P-051","records":["seabornObit","parentage","muster","parole","homestead","escoParentage"],"body":"Seaborn was a farmer near Warnerton and Christine’s grandfather. Census and family accounts identify him as John and Margaret’s son. He served in the Confederate cavalry, was captured at Port Hudson in 1863 and was released on parole.\n\nHe married Laura Ellzey in 1866. They had ten children, according to his obituary; Esco, Christine’s father, was one of them. After Laura’s death, Seaborn married Candacy Bankston and had three more children.\n\nHis homestead lay south of his father’s land. When he died in 1919, his obituary said his home was “in sight of the place of his birth.”","html":"<p>Seaborn was a farmer near Warnerton and Christine’s grandfather. Census and family accounts identify him as John and Margaret’s son. He served in the Confederate cavalry, was captured at Port Hudson in 1863 and was released on parole.<\/p>\n<p>He married Laura Ellzey in 1866. They had ten children, according to his obituary; Esco, Christine’s father, was one of them. After Laura’s death, Seaborn married Candacy Bankston and had three more children.<\/p>\n<p>His homestead lay south of his father’s land. When he died in 1919, his obituary said his home was “in sight of the place of his birth.”<\/p>"},"john":{"id":"john","name":"John Seaborn Alford","dates":"1807–1891 or 1897","relationship":"Christine’s great-grandfather","archiveKey":"john-seaborn-alford","records":["twin","parentage","margaret","patents","field","farm","landscape"],"body":"John was a Washington Parish farmer and Christine’s great-grandfather. He married Margaret in 1833, and they raised a large family. Their land lay near the Bogue Chitto River; one of John’s lots later contained the family cemetery.\n\nHe had a twin brother, Seaborn John, who became a Methodist preacher. John’s own son was also named Seaborn. Census and family accounts support the relationship from John and Margaret to this younger Seaborn, Christine’s grandfather.\n\nThe twins’ later family account names their father Jacob, but we have not securely connected him to Julius Alford’s son in North Carolina. John’s death year is also unsettled: later sources give 1891 and 1897.","html":"<p>John was a Washington Parish farmer and Christine’s great-grandfather. He married Margaret in 1833, and they raised a large family. Their land lay near the Bogue Chitto River; one of John’s lots later contained the family cemetery.<\/p>\n<p>He had a twin brother, Seaborn John, who became a Methodist preacher. John’s own son was also named Seaborn. Census and family accounts support the relationship from John and Margaret to this younger Seaborn, Christine’s grandfather.<\/p>\n<p>The twins’ later family account names their father Jacob, but we have not securely connected him to Julius Alford’s son in North Carolina. John’s death year is also unsettled: later sources give 1891 and 1897.<\/p>"}},"records":{"portHudsonCamp":{"id":"portHudsonCamp","title":"A camp at Port Hudson","citation":"McPherson & Oliver · May 1863–1865 · Library of Congress","refs":"LOC 2015647757 · LC-DIG-stereo-1s04334","image":"assets/port-hudson-camp.jpg","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2015647757/","caption":"Camp at Port Hudson · May 1863–1865 · McPherson & Oliver / Library of Congress","body":"The Library of Congress identifies this stereograph as soldiers in camp during the siege of Port Hudson and dates it between May 1863 and 1865. It shows people, tents and camp equipment beneath trees.\n\nThe catalog does not identify Seaborn or his unit. The Library of Congress lists no known restrictions on publication.","html":"<p>The Library of Congress identifies this stereograph as soldiers in camp during the siege of Port Hudson and dates it between May 1863 and 1865. It shows people, tents and camp equipment beneath trees.<\/p>\n<p>The catalog does not identify Seaborn or his unit. The Library of Congress lists no known restrictions on publication.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/port-hudson-camp.jpg","dimensions":[5514,3043],"displayBytes":190167,"sourceSha256":"ecefcc87d99f0908d8c936bcea0f81daa3d3168c43bc7eefbc517b0b204486c1"},"census1930":{"id":"census1930","title":"The Alford household in 1930","citation":"Washington Parish · Ward 2 · ED 59-2 · sheet 2A, lines 19–22","refs":"S-1638; C-208","image":"assets/census-1930-excerpt.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Alford household · 1930 census · assembled excerpt with original headings","body":"The household includes Esco, 52; Lou, 47; Tolbert, 16; and Christine, 9. Christine is identified as a daughter and is attending school. These are the ages the census taker recorded.\n\nThe displayed image joins the original column headings to the four Alford household rows, numbered 19–22. Other rows have been omitted. It is an assembled excerpt, rather than one continuous section of the page.","html":"<p>The household includes Esco, 52; Lou, 47; Tolbert, 16; and Christine, 9. Christine is identified as a daughter and is attending school. These are the ages the census taker recorded.<\/p>\n<p>The displayed image joins the original column headings to the four Alford household rows, numbered 19–22. Other rows have been omitted. It is an assembled excerpt, rather than one continuous section of the page.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/census-1930-excerpt.jpg","dimensions":[3680,747],"displayBytes":124451,"sourceSha256":"4f16720562c8a6b3ae69973794774282ebdd133308dfcf8edfbd765ecad5ad4e"},"margaret":{"id":"margaret","title":"Margaret Alford’s obituary","citation":"Magee’s Creek Baptist Association minutes · 1885","refs":"S-1869; S-1826","image":"assets/margaret-obituary.jpeg","url":null,"caption":"Margaret Alford · Magee’s Creek Baptist Association minutes · 1885","body":"Margaret’s obituary appears in the 1885 minutes of the Magee’s Creek Baptist Association. It reports her birth in Washington Parish on 9 February 1819, marriage to John S. Alford in 1833 and death on 8 September 1885.\n\nIt recounts her Methodist membership from 1853, baptism into Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866 and later move to Bogue Chitto church. The husband, fourteen children and forty-three grandchildren it names as mourners are survivors at her death, rather than a count of every child born.\n\nThe displayed extract joins the end of one page to the continuation on the next. The early birth and marriage dates are later recollections in this church account. It calls her Margaret Alford; it does not give her maiden name.","html":"<p>Margaret’s obituary appears in the 1885 minutes of the Magee’s Creek Baptist Association. It reports her birth in Washington Parish on 9 February 1819, marriage to John S. Alford in 1833 and death on 8 September 1885.<\/p>\n<p>It recounts her Methodist membership from 1853, baptism into Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866 and later move to Bogue Chitto church. The husband, fourteen children and forty-three grandchildren it names as mourners are survivors at her death, rather than a count of every child born.<\/p>\n<p>The displayed extract joins the end of one page to the continuation on the next. The early birth and marriage dates are later recollections in this church account. It calls her Margaret Alford; it does not give her maiden name.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/margaret-obituary.jpg","dimensions":[539,679],"displayBytes":82255,"sourceSha256":"aa1641999dd3ccd970f9823d09c10513f5c2642bb61a7d025964b21d24a1416d"},"portHudsonBattery":{"id":"portHudsonBattery","title":"Union artillery at Port Hudson","citation":"McPherson & Oliver · 1863 · Library of Congress","refs":"LOC 2015647751 · LC-DIG-stereo-1s04328","image":"assets/port-hudson-battery.jpg","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2015647751/","caption":"Union artillery at Port Hudson · 1863 · McPherson & Oliver / Library of Congress","body":"The Library of Congress identifies this stereograph as Union sailors at Battery 10 during the siege of Port Hudson, in May and June 1863.\n\nIt shows the forces opposing Seaborn’s Confederate side. No person pictured is identified as an Alford. The Library of Congress lists no known restrictions on publication.","html":"<p>The Library of Congress identifies this stereograph as Union sailors at Battery 10 during the siege of Port Hudson, in May and June 1863.<\/p>\n<p>It shows the forces opposing Seaborn’s Confederate side. No person pictured is identified as an Alford. The Library of Congress lists no known restrictions on publication.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/port-hudson-battery.jpg","dimensions":[5409,2959],"displayBytes":174590,"sourceSha256":"1edd130eb9fdceb02a735182bd14bd17ef2456974145dde7d7d44e8b13372d4e"},"census1900":{"id":"census1900","title":"Esco and Mary Lou’s 1900 household","citation":"Washington Parish · 1900 federal census","refs":"S-1292; alford-direct-line-land-tenure-2026-09-08/findings.md","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The 1900 census records Esco as the owner of his farm and gives Mary L.’s birth as March 1883. His father and brother appear nearby in the census taker’s sequence of households.\n\nThat sequence suggests nearby homes, but it does not establish distances or property boundaries. The mortgage column is unclear in this return; the 1910 return records Esco’s farm as free of mortgage.","html":"<p>The 1900 census records Esco as the owner of his farm and gives Mary L.’s birth as March 1883. His father and brother appear nearby in the census taker’s sequence of households.<\/p>\n<p>That sequence suggests nearby homes, but it does not establish distances or property boundaries. The mortgage column is unclear in this return; the 1910 return records Esco’s farm as free of mortgage.<\/p>"},"seabornObit":{"id":"seabornObit","title":"“The End Of A Long And Useful Life”","citation":"The Era-Leader · 4 December 1919, page 2","refs":"S-1654; C-211; C-212","image":"assets/seaborn-obituary-1919.png","url":null,"caption":"Seaborn L. Alford’s obituary · The Era-Leader · 4 December 1919","body":"W. F. Hutson’s obituary gives Seaborn’s birth as 19 May 1844 and death as 22 November 1919. It recounts his marriage to Laura A. Ellzy in 1866, ten children with Laura, a second marriage to Candacy Bankston in 1909 and three children from that marriage.\n\nIt places his home three miles south of Warnerton, “in sight of the place of his birth.” This is a later account of his early life, written at his death.","html":"<p>W. F. Hutson’s obituary gives Seaborn’s birth as 19 May 1844 and death as 22 November 1919. It recounts his marriage to Laura A. Ellzy in 1866, ten children with Laura, a second marriage to Candacy Bankston in 1909 and three children from that marriage.<\/p>\n<p>It places his home three miles south of Warnerton, “in sight of the place of his birth.” This is a later account of his early life, written at his death.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/seaborn-obituary-1919.jpg","dimensions":[1600,2712],"displayBytes":483629,"sourceSha256":"e5b7d9104c2e6210709a8e5bad2f97b91638c7a4e7ca24477aa75deba99aa4c5"},"census1950":{"id":"census1950","title":"Christine and Charles in the 1950 census","citation":"Baton Rouge · adult household entries · image 18, lines 15–16","refs":"S-1861","image":null,"url":null,"body":"Charles B. Knight, 33, is recorded as an assistant manager at a funeral home. Christine A., 29, is a registered nurse working in parish health. Her entry gives a forty-hour workweek and government employment.\n\nThe display transcribes their adult entries. The complete page includes a living descendant and is not reproduced.","html":"<p>Charles B. Knight, 33, is recorded as an assistant manager at a funeral home. Christine A., 29, is a registered nurse working in parish health. Her entry gives a forty-hour workweek and government employment.<\/p>\n<p>The display transcribes their adult entries. The complete page includes a living descendant and is not reproduced.<\/p>"},"muster":{"id":"muster","title":"S. L. Alford’s muster card","citation":"National Archives · M320 · 9th Battalion Louisiana Partisan Rangers","refs":"S-1808; S-1809","image":"assets/muster-1862.jpg","url":null,"body":"Seaborn enlisted at Camp Moore on 13 May 1862, in Company K of the Confederate 9th Battalion Louisiana Partisan Rangers. The unit later became Wingfield’s 3rd Louisiana Cavalry.\n\nThe image is a card from his compiled military service record, assembled from military records.","html":"<p>Seaborn enlisted at Camp Moore on 13 May 1862, in Company K of the Confederate 9th Battalion Louisiana Partisan Rangers. The unit later became Wingfield’s 3rd Louisiana Cavalry.<\/p>\n<p>The image is a card from his compiled military service record, assembled from military records.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/muster-1862.jpg","dimensions":[1196,2685],"displayBytes":90178,"sourceSha256":"594da964a8d85d26f246d0f098e82a9853bfe14a03837a58739a03b77bf41a34"},"parole":{"id":"parole","title":"Port Hudson prisoner-of-war card","citation":"National Archives · captured 9 July 1863; paroled 12–13 July","refs":"S-1808; S-1809","image":"assets/parole-1863.jpg","url":null,"body":"Seaborn’s service record places his capture at Port Hudson on 9 July 1863. Its cards give parole dates of 12–13 July; they do not settle one exact day.\n\nThe image is a card compiled from military records, rather than a photograph of the original parole roll.","html":"<p>Seaborn’s service record places his capture at Port Hudson on 9 July 1863. Its cards give parole dates of 12–13 July; they do not settle one exact day.<\/p>\n<p>The image is a card compiled from military records, rather than a photograph of the original parole roll.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/parole-1863.jpg","dimensions":[1196,2638],"displayBytes":88081,"sourceSha256":"28a89a25561b25e97f5b0812fc84b5d7e84610f9bdb9bec7aaa9ab9d2088187f"},"will":{"id":"will","title":"Julius Alford’s will","citation":"Bute County, North Carolina · written 14 July 1768, proved November 1771","refs":"C-139; S-1130; S-1711","image":"assets/julius-will-1768.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Julius Alford’s will · North Carolina · 1768 · FamilySearch","body":"Julius’s will names his wife Lucy and seven children: John, Isaac, Goodrich, Jacob, Job, Patty and Sarah. It provides for maintaining and schooling the children and for Lucy’s use of residual property during her lifetime or widowhood.\n\nJacob was to receive the home plantation on both sides of the Tar River, along with livestock and a breeding mare. The river word is faint in the manuscript; the displayed transcript retains the uncertain reading as “[Tar].”\n\nThe will was written on 14 July 1768 and proved in November 1771—accepted by the court after Julius’s death. It establishes this North Carolina family, but does not connect Jacob to the later Louisiana Alfords.","html":"<p>Julius’s will names his wife Lucy and seven children: John, Isaac, Goodrich, Jacob, Job, Patty and Sarah. It provides for maintaining and schooling the children and for Lucy’s use of residual property during her lifetime or widowhood.<\/p>\n<p>Jacob was to receive the home plantation on both sides of the Tar River, along with livestock and a breeding mare. The river word is faint in the manuscript; the displayed transcript retains the uncertain reading as “[Tar].”<\/p>\n<p>The will was written on 14 July 1768 and proved in November 1771—accepted by the court after Julius’s death. It establishes this North Carolina family, but does not connect Jacob to the later Louisiana Alfords.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/julius-will-1768.jpg","dimensions":[3073,4554],"displayBytes":298812,"sourceSha256":"3c988d0d8f67cdd39cb4a6eb3fd8565b7747293cdef51e9907165a1811af1d0c"},"johnPortrait":{"id":"johnPortrait","title":"Portrait attributed to John Seaborn Alford","citation":"AAFA attribution · photograph undated","refs":"S-1869","image":"assets/john-portrait.jpg","url":null,"body":"This portrait was uploaded to John Seaborn Alford’s Find a Grave memorial by Pamela Thompson in 2013 and credited to the Alford American Family Association archives. The photograph itself does not name the man shown, so the identification remains an attribution.","html":"<p>This portrait was uploaded to John Seaborn Alford’s Find a Grave memorial by Pamela Thompson in 2013 and credited to the Alford American Family Association archives. The photograph itself does not name the man shown, so the identification remains an attribution.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/john-portrait.jpg","dimensions":[2085,2934],"displayBytes":194238,"sourceSha256":"7a1b30d3f9d3ac5c3522b26ae93f556bd6646da9413b3b4a2f13e38ab5936e8b"},"landscape":{"id":"landscape","title":"How this landscape was made","citation":"Geography: docs/research-intake/alford-story-2026-09-22/atlas/alford-atlas.geojson, web/atlas-data.json, PLAT-READINGS.md §§1–5. The river’s disconnected source segments remain disconnected. Family connections retain the source qualifications in the notes above.","refs":"Geography: docs/research-intake/alford-story-2026-09-22/atlas/alford-atlas.geojson, web/atlas-data.json, PLAT-READINGS.md §§1–5. The river’s disconnected source segments remain disconnected. Family connections retain the source qualifications in the notes above.","image":null,"body":"The map places historical land claims and grants beside the modern Bogue Chitto River. Their outlines were reconstructed from the 1851 township map and present-day section boundaries. Small boundaries are approximate, generally within about fifty metres; houses and travel routes have not been plotted.\n\nJacob’s claim and Seaborn’s homestead lie about three and a half miles apart, measured between their centres. The family’s land records span several township sections.\n\nColours distinguish John’s and Seaborn’s properties. The broken outline around Jacob’s claim marks a possible earlier family connection. The accumulating outlines compare land records from different years; they do not show uninterrupted ownership or inheritance of one farm.","html":"<p>The map places historical land claims and grants beside the modern Bogue Chitto River. Their outlines were reconstructed from the 1851 township map and present-day section boundaries. Small boundaries are approximate, generally within about fifty metres; houses and travel routes have not been plotted.<\/p>\n<p>Jacob’s claim and Seaborn’s homestead lie about three and a half miles apart, measured between their centres. The family’s land records span several township sections.<\/p>\n<p>Colours distinguish John’s and Seaborn’s properties. The broken outline around Jacob’s claim marks a possible earlier family connection. The accumulating outlines compare land records from different years; they do not show uninterrupted ownership or inheritance of one farm.<\/p>"},"escoMarriage":{"id":"escoMarriage","title":"Esco Alford’s marriages","citation":"Compiled Louisiana marriage indexes: Esco Alford and Mary L. Fussell, 28 December 1899; Esco S. Alford and Lynnie Schilling, 30 April 1946; Esco Alford and Florence Bennett, 23 May 1951. Lynnie death evidence: S-1828.","refs":"S-1842; S-1828; C-186","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The compiled marriage index records Esco Alford and Mary L. Fussell on 28 December 1899. It also records his marriages to Lynnie Schilling on 30 April 1946 and Florence Bennett on 23 May 1951. These dates have not yet been checked against the original marriage returns.\n\nMary Lou died in February 1946; Lynnie’s death is recorded in 1947. These later marriages help explain references to Mrs. E. S. Alford after Mary Lou’s death.\n\nMary Lou’s death-index entries name Henry T. Fussell as her father and Eliza or Liza Hobbs as her mother. Other family entries conflict over the mother’s name, so that identification remains qualified.","html":"<p>The compiled marriage index records Esco Alford and Mary L. Fussell on 28 December 1899. It also records his marriages to Lynnie Schilling on 30 April 1946 and Florence Bennett on 23 May 1951. These dates have not yet been checked against the original marriage returns.<\/p>\n<p>Mary Lou died in February 1946; Lynnie’s death is recorded in 1947. These later marriages help explain references to Mrs. E. S. Alford after Mary Lou’s death.<\/p>\n<p>Mary Lou’s death-index entries name Henry T. Fussell as her father and Eliza or Liza Hobbs as her mother. Other family entries conflict over the mother’s name, so that identification remains qualified.<\/p>"},"plat":{"id":"plat","title":"The 1851 township map","citation":"Archive: atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §§1–5; 1851-cabell-dependent-resurvey-document-6016369610341538134.pdf in the Section 54 package’s sources/pre-1939-maps/glo directory. Map placement is approximate, generally about ±50 metres.","refs":"Archive: atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §§1–5; 1851-cabell-dependent-resurvey-document-6016369610341538134.pdf in the Section 54 package’s sources/pre-1939-maps/glo directory. Map placement is approximate, generally about ±50 metres.","image":"assets/plat-1851.jpg","caption":"Township 1 South, Range 10 East · 1851 General Land Office plat","body":"This government map, approved in September 1851, divides the township into older private claims and smaller numbered lots. Its lot numbers and acreages allow John’s and Seaborn’s later land grants to be located.\n\nThe image is the archive’s rendition of the General Land Office township map. Modern placement of the small boundaries is approximate, generally within about fifty metres.","html":"<p>This government map, approved in September 1851, divides the township into older private claims and smaller numbered lots. Its lot numbers and acreages allow John’s and Seaborn’s later land grants to be located.<\/p>\n<p>The image is the archive’s rendition of the General Land Office township map. Modern placement of the small boundaries is approximate, generally within about fifty metres.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/plat-1851.jpg","dimensions":[1800,1801],"displayBytes":435663,"sourceSha256":"19552f2b549148c20629907c1b38d1d7aea15ea13b0ecbd157d00a01747f3372"},"directory":{"id":"directory","title":"Christine in the Baton Rouge directories","citation":"1942, page 32; 1943, page 27","refs":"S-1256; C-184","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The 1942 and 1943 Baton Rouge city directories both list Christine Alford as a nurse for the Parish Health Unit. They place her in public-health work soon after her graduation from Touro.\n\nWhere directory wording is displayed, it is a transcription of the entries, rather than an image of the original page.","html":"<p>The 1942 and 1943 Baton Rouge city directories both list Christine Alford as a nurse for the Parish Health Unit. They place her in public-health work soon after her graduation from Touro.<\/p>\n<p>Where directory wording is displayed, it is a transcription of the entries, rather than an image of the original page.<\/p>"},"patents":{"id":"patents","title":"John S. Alford’s land patents","citation":"General Land Office · 1858, 1861, 1884","refs":"S-1670; S-1810","image":null,"url":null,"body":"John received federal title to 93.84 acres in 1858: 57.24 acres in section 17 and 36.60 acres in section 18. Later entries added 22.28 acres in 1861 and 80.57 acres in 1884.\n\nThese grants identify land acquired on particular dates. They do not establish how long John kept each parcel or who inherited it.","html":"<p>John received federal title to 93.84 acres in 1858: 57.24 acres in section 17 and 36.60 acres in section 18. Later entries added 22.28 acres in 1861 and 80.57 acres in 1884.<\/p>\n<p>These grants identify land acquired on particular dates. They do not establish how long John kept each parcel or who inherited it.<\/p>"},"escoDraft":{"id":"escoDraft","title":"Esco Seaborn Alford’s draft registration","citation":"12 September 1918 · First World War registration","refs":"S-1292","image":"assets/esco-draft-1918.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Esco’s draft registration · 1918 · registration does not establish service","body":"Esco completed this registration in 1918. It gives his full name and his own birth date as 21 May 1878.\n\nRegistering for the draft does not by itself show that he served in the military. The card is used here for his personal details.","html":"<p>Esco completed this registration in 1918. It gives his full name and his own birth date as 21 May 1878.<\/p>\n<p>Registering for the draft does not by itself show that he served in the military. The card is used here for his personal details.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/esco-draft-1918.jpg","dimensions":[1672,920],"displayBytes":172397,"sourceSha256":"c87350ed992c61b1d3ecc693a22ccc04d8fc6d0dbc7fc94af77b1ca48f77c3e9"},"laura":{"id":"laura","title":"Laura A. Alford’s gravestone","citation":"Seaborn Alford Cemetery · held Find a Grave photograph","refs":"C-191; C-212; C-213; S-1654; S-1826; S-1866","image":"assets/laura-grave.png","url":null,"caption":"Laura A. Alford’s marker · undated photograph via Find a Grave · dates read from a separate transcription","body":"Laura’s marker is weathered, and its dates cannot be read reliably in this photograph. A cemetery transcription made in 1965 gives 25 April 1844–19 March 1903. A family Bible abstract also gives her birth date.\n\nSeaborn’s obituary calls his first wife Laura A. Ellzy and dates their marriage to 24 December 1866. The evidence supports, but does not establish, her identification as John and Elizabeth Ellzey’s daughter.","html":"<p>Laura’s marker is weathered, and its dates cannot be read reliably in this photograph. A cemetery transcription made in 1965 gives 25 April 1844–19 March 1903. A family Bible abstract also gives her birth date.<\/p>\n<p>Seaborn’s obituary calls his first wife Laura A. Ellzy and dates their marriage to 24 December 1866. The evidence supports, but does not establish, her identification as John and Elizabeth Ellzey’s daughter.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/laura-grave.jpg","dimensions":[1152,2048],"displayBytes":235950,"sourceSha256":"fd248cc5cd041f6960231428c0dd43c21fff6e0d1d94258383cb4f811146aa1f"},"escoParentage":{"id":"escoParentage","title":"The 1880 family household","citation":"Washington Parish · Seaborn L. and Laura Alford","refs":"C-190; C-191","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The 1880 household names two-year-old “Escoe” as Seaborn’s son. Laura is in the household as Seaborn’s wife.\n\nThe evidence establishes Seaborn as Esco’s father and supports Laura as his mother through this household and related family records.","html":"<p>The 1880 household names two-year-old “Escoe” as Seaborn’s son. Laura is in the household as Seaborn’s wife.<\/p>\n<p>The evidence establishes Seaborn as Esco’s father and supports Laura as his mother through this household and related family records.<\/p>"},"escoPortrait":{"id":"escoPortrait","title":"Esco Alford’s newspaper portrait","citation":"The Era-Leader · 24 April 1958, page 1","refs":"S-1806","image":"assets/esco-portrait-caption.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Esco Alford · The Era-Leader · 24 April 1958 · portrait and caption","body":"The newspaper printed Esco’s portrait after his death and described his farming, church and civic work. His description as a deacon comes from this later caption, rather than the earlier funeral notice.","html":"<p>The newspaper printed Esco’s portrait after his death and described his farming, church and civic work. His description as a deacon comes from this later caption, rather than the earlier funeral notice.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/esco-portrait-caption.jpg","dimensions":[405,1160],"displayBytes":93863,"sourceSha256":"dc6bd04a5e809645c0948a921de31bdb4ca521af25ecc5249130fc288f61c876"},"nursing":{"id":"nursing","title":"Christine’s 1941 graduation notice","citation":"The Bogalusa News, 29 August 1941, printed Page Ten, “Mt. Hermon Events.”","refs":"S-1017; C-112","image":"assets/nursing-1941.jpg","url":null,"caption":"“Mt. Hermon Events” · The Bogalusa News · 29 August 1941 · Page Ten","body":"The notice reports Christine’s graduation from Touro Infirmary’s school of nursing on 20 August 1941. It says she was at home for a three-week vacation before returning to nurse at Touro. The next item reports Esco’s trip to New Orleans to bring her home.\n\nThe graduation notice calls her the daughter of “Mr. and Mrs. Asco Alford.” The next item spells her father’s name “Esco.” Both printed spellings are preserved here.\n\nThe newspaper’s printed page is Page Ten. The saved provider file uses a different image sequence number.","html":"<p>The notice reports Christine’s graduation from Touro Infirmary’s school of nursing on 20 August 1941. It says she was at home for a three-week vacation before returning to nurse at Touro. The next item reports Esco’s trip to New Orleans to bring her home.<\/p>\n<p>The graduation notice calls her the daughter of “Mr. and Mrs. Asco Alford.” The next item spells her father’s name “Esco.” Both printed spellings are preserved here.<\/p>\n<p>The newspaper’s printed page is Page Ten. The saved provider file uses a different image sequence number.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/nursing-1941.jpg","dimensions":[2716,3769],"displayBytes":330201,"sourceSha256":"b1f0b6f898890935f49ea4c9fbf31bf655b8161517fd7317157a6a4d60d05aee"},"twin":{"id":"twin","title":"Seaborn J. Alford’s obituary","citation":"New Orleans Christian Advocate · 12 June 1884","refs":"C-140; S-1134","image":"assets/seaborn-obituary-1884.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Seaborn J. Alford’s obituary · 1884 · New Orleans Christian Advocate","body":"The 1884 obituary of Seaborn J. Alford describes him and John S. as twins, born on 11 October 1807 in Louisiana near present-day Osyka, Mississippi. It says their father Jacob came from South Carolina and died while they were children. Their mother is not named.\n\nIt also describes Seaborn J.’s Methodist life and work as a local preacher. The evidence supports identifying his twin John with Christine’s great-grandfather.\n\nThis account was written decades after the twins’ birth. It does not establish that their father was Julius Alford’s son or the Jacob of the Bogue Chitto claim.","html":"<p>The 1884 obituary of Seaborn J. Alford describes him and John S. as twins, born on 11 October 1807 in Louisiana near present-day Osyka, Mississippi. It says their father Jacob came from South Carolina and died while they were children. Their mother is not named.<\/p>\n<p>It also describes Seaborn J.’s Methodist life and work as a local preacher. The evidence supports identifying his twin John with Christine’s great-grandfather.<\/p>\n<p>This account was written decades after the twins’ birth. It does not establish that their father was Julius Alford’s son or the Jacob of the Bogue Chitto claim.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/seaborn-obituary-1884.jpg","dimensions":[650,1700],"displayBytes":300959,"sourceSha256":"ef5febb8941470b008867e5ec66a52b1abc2a0653cf35a2534edb78cbe5efb3e"},"survey":{"id":"survey","title":"Survey for the heirs of Jacob Alford","citation":"20 January 1831 · Bogue Chitto · certificate 41","refs":"C-170; S-1227; S-1135","image":"assets/jacob-heirs-survey-1831.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Survey for Jacob Alford’s heirs · 20 January 1831 · FamilySearch","body":"The survey of 20 January 1831 records 636.54 acres for the heirs of Jacob Alford, beside the Bogue Chitto River. The claim originated with an earlier settlement claim. Jacob was dead by the time of this survey, but the heirs are not named.\n\nHe may have been the Jacob named as John and Seaborn’s father in the later family account. That identification, and a connection to Julius’s North Carolina son, remain unproved.\n\nThe outline on the modern map is a later reconstruction of the claim. The original survey drawing is shown separately, in its own proportions.","html":"<p>The survey of 20 January 1831 records 636.54 acres for the heirs of Jacob Alford, beside the Bogue Chitto River. The claim originated with an earlier settlement claim. Jacob was dead by the time of this survey, but the heirs are not named.<\/p>\n<p>He may have been the Jacob named as John and Seaborn’s father in the later family account. That identification, and a connection to Julius’s North Carolina son, remain unproved.<\/p>\n<p>The outline on the modern map is a later reconstruction of the claim. The original survey drawing is shown separately, in its own proportions.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/jacob-heirs-survey-1831.jpg","dimensions":[2555,3788],"displayBytes":277716,"sourceSha256":"71afa006ca4b8d83d1e049410d59bfbc75b75fcacf942d9e69c54be9ca8c9104"},"field":{"id":"field","title":"The field before the patent","citation":"Archive: atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §§3, 5; S-1810, John S. Alford document 1717; S-1612 (1850 household). The documentary crop retains the neighbouring claim boundary and the lot’s acreage for context.","refs":"Archive: atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §§3, 5; S-1810, John S. Alford document 1717; S-1612 (1850 household). The documentary crop retains the neighbouring claim boundary and the lot’s acreage for context.","image":"assets/plat-1851.jpg","body":"The 1851 map marks FIELD beside the older Elisha Roberts claim. The drawn field extends into lot 1 of section 18, a 36.60-acre lot included in John’s 1858 land grant.\n\nThe map shows that the ground was being worked before the grant. It does not name the farmer. John’s presence in the parish by 1850 does not, by itself, identify the field as his.","html":"<p>The 1851 map marks FIELD beside the older Elisha Roberts claim. The drawn field extends into lot 1 of section 18, a 36.60-acre lot included in John’s 1858 land grant.<\/p>\n<p>The map shows that the ground was being worked before the grant. It does not name the farmer. John’s presence in the parish by 1850 does not, by itself, identify the field as his.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/plat-1851.jpg","dimensions":[1800,1801],"displayBytes":435663,"sourceSha256":"19552f2b549148c20629907c1b38d1d7aea15ea13b0ecbd157d00a01747f3372"},"escoObit":{"id":"escoObit","title":"Esco’s funeral in the hometown paper","citation":"The Era-Leader · 17 April 1958, page 1","refs":"S-1805; C-112","image":"assets/esco-obituary-1958.jpg","url":null,"body":"The notice describes Esco as a retired farmer and dairyman who died at Tylertown Hospital after a stroke. It names Christine Knight of Baton Rouge among his children and gives Bogue Chitto church cemetery as his burial place.\n\nThe likely death date, 15 April 1958, is calculated from the notice’s “Tuesday” and the 17 April issue date. The notice does not print that calendar date.","html":"<p>The notice describes Esco as a retired farmer and dairyman who died at Tylertown Hospital after a stroke. It names Christine Knight of Baton Rouge among his children and gives Bogue Chitto church cemetery as his burial place.<\/p>\n<p>The likely death date, 15 April 1958, is calculated from the notice’s “Tuesday” and the 17 April issue date. The notice does not print that calendar date.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/esco-obituary-1958.jpg","dimensions":[580,1350],"displayBytes":164530,"sourceSha256":"be41533ae56d53064b42c21a3d4a0fe58b28c40a412d6be5471955b68618ccf0"},"portHudsonWorks":{"id":"portHudsonWorks","title":"Confederate fortifications at Port Hudson","citation":"McPherson & Oliver · July 1863–1865 · Library of Congress","refs":"LOC 2015647754 · LC-DIG-stereo-1s04331","image":"assets/port-hudson-fortifications.jpg","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2015647754/","caption":"Port Hudson fortifications · July 1863–1865 · McPherson & Oliver / Library of Congress","body":"The Library of Congress dates this view of damaged Confederate fortifications to between July 1863 and 1865. It shows timber, sandbags and earthworks at Port Hudson.\n\nIt illustrates the Confederate position, but we cannot place Seaborn at this particular spot. The story uses part of the left-hand image; the viewer opens the complete stereoscopic card. The Library of Congress lists no known restrictions on publication.","html":"<p>The Library of Congress dates this view of damaged Confederate fortifications to between July 1863 and 1865. It shows timber, sandbags and earthworks at Port Hudson.<\/p>\n<p>It illustrates the Confederate position, but we cannot place Seaborn at this particular spot. The story uses part of the left-hand image; the viewer opens the complete stereoscopic card. The Library of Congress lists no known restrictions on publication.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/port-hudson-fortifications.jpg","dimensions":[5504,3017],"displayBytes":160595,"sourceSha256":"11309fa36a8e38d4f21911ec22974514e9dec33d578c2c0410820040796b5250"},"parentage":{"id":"parentage","title":"John and Margaret → Seaborn L.","citation":"1860 household; family Bible copy; later family account","refs":"C-209; S-1643; S-1132","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The evidence supports John and Margaret as Seaborn L.’s parents. Their 1860 household includes sixteen-year-old Seaborne L. A family Bible copy and a later account of his naming strengthen that identification.\n\nThe 1860 census did not record household relationships. The 1850 household also has conflicting ages, so the identification rests on the combined evidence rather than one census entry.","html":"<p>The evidence supports John and Margaret as Seaborn L.’s parents. Their 1860 household includes sixteen-year-old Seaborne L. A family Bible copy and a later account of his naming strengthen that identification.<\/p>\n<p>The 1860 census did not record household relationships. The 1850 household also has conflicting ages, so the identification rests on the combined evidence rather than one census entry.<\/p>"},"touroPhoto":{"id":"touroPhoto","title":"Touro Infirmary, circa 1910","citation":"Detroit Publishing Co. · circa 1910 · Library of Congress via Wikimedia Commons","refs":"LOC det.4a23672 · Commons TouroInfirmary1910.jpg","image":"assets/touro-infirmary-c1910.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TouroInfirmary1910.jpg","caption":"Touro Infirmary · circa 1910 · Detroit Publishing Co. / Library of Congress via Wikimedia Commons","body":"This view shows Touro Infirmary on Prytania Street in New Orleans around 1910, roughly thirty years before Christine’s graduation. It is a view of the institution, rather than of Christine or her class.\n\nWikimedia Commons identifies the Library of Congress source as det.4a23672 and marks the photograph as public domain in the United States.","html":"<p>This view shows Touro Infirmary on Prytania Street in New Orleans around 1910, roughly thirty years before Christine’s graduation. It is a view of the institution, rather than of Christine or her class.<\/p>\n<p>Wikimedia Commons identifies the Library of Congress source as det.4a23672 and marks the photograph as public domain in the United States.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/touro-infirmary-c1910.jpg","dimensions":[4200,3354],"displayBytes":285124,"sourceSha256":"584d080e11e0c0c86e6d1e6935022eacf07625a930011be8eded7ddd6eb7a55e"},"marriage":{"id":"marriage","title":"Christine Alford and Charles B. Knight","citation":"Louisiana marriage index · 1 February 1946 · Bienville Parish","refs":"C-113; S-1021","image":"assets/marriage-1946.jpg","url":null,"body":"The Louisiana marriage index records Christine Alford and Charles B. Knight on 1 February 1946 in Bienville Parish. Other records identify him as Charles Binford Knight.\n\nThe original licence and marriage return have not yet been examined. The story uses the date given in the index.","html":"<p>The Louisiana marriage index records Christine Alford and Charles B. Knight on 1 February 1946 in Bienville Parish. Other records identify him as Charles Binford Knight.<\/p>\n<p>The original licence and marriage return have not yet been examined. The story uses the date given in the index.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/marriage-1946.jpg","dimensions":[1920,952],"displayBytes":207930,"sourceSha256":"4943019d6ac961a9685117de958b4923b289ff855bc9d78ecca7070c6f8ea91b"},"deed":{"id":"deed","title":"Jacob Alford’s deed","citation":"Franklin County, North Carolina · 1789 · deed book 7, page 41","refs":"C-139; S-1131","image":"assets/jacob-deed-1789.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Franklin County deed · 1789 · FamilySearch","body":"In this 1789 agreement with Simon Williams, Jacob sells his right in Julius’s estate, due to him after his mother’s death. It concerns an inheritance, rather than a sale of a particular tract of land. He calls himself the son of the deceased Julius Alford.\n\nTogether with Julius’s will, it establishes the father-and-son relationship in North Carolina.\n\nIt does not identify Jacob with the man later named as the Louisiana twins’ father.","html":"<p>In this 1789 agreement with Simon Williams, Jacob sells his right in Julius’s estate, due to him after his mother’s death. It concerns an inheritance, rather than a sale of a particular tract of land. He calls himself the son of the deceased Julius Alford.<\/p>\n<p>Together with Julius’s will, it establishes the father-and-son relationship in North Carolina.<\/p>\n<p>It does not identify Jacob with the man later named as the Louisiana twins’ father.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/jacob-deed-1789.jpg","dimensions":[4400,3050],"displayBytes":428365,"sourceSha256":"3fc82cdd13822ab6f7775a4bd30d19366fff67fa964a2f2a99cced52e33e31e7"},"memorial":{"id":"memorial","title":"Christine Alford Knight’s memorial","citation":"Obituary clipping preserved in AAFA member file 0741 · 2008","refs":"AAFA-0741-and-John-Seaborn-recovery-2026-08-27; C-112; C-208","image":null,"url":null,"body":"Christine’s later obituary describes her farm birth near Mount Hermon, parents Esco and Mary Lou, nursing education, marriage and public-health career. It reports a 1941 Touro nursing degree, a 1944 “CPH degree” from Peabody College, thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department and retirement in 1975.\n\nAn original birth record and contemporary Peabody graduation record have not been examined. Accounts also disagree on her exact death date, so the story uses 2008 without choosing a day.\n\nThe obituary is used for selected biographical details. The complete notice names living descendants and is not reproduced.","html":"<p>Christine’s later obituary describes her farm birth near Mount Hermon, parents Esco and Mary Lou, nursing education, marriage and public-health career. It reports a 1941 Touro nursing degree, a 1944 “CPH degree” from Peabody College, thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department and retirement in 1975.<\/p>\n<p>An original birth record and contemporary Peabody graduation record have not been examined. Accounts also disagree on her exact death date, so the story uses 2008 without choosing a day.<\/p>\n<p>The obituary is used for selected biographical details. The complete notice names living descendants and is not reproduced.<\/p>"},"john":{"id":"john","title":"John’s lots on the map","citation":"Archive: S-1670; S-1810; atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §3. GLO documents 1718 (57.24 acres) and 1717 (36.60 acres), 1 June 1858; subsequent entry, 10 May 1861. The displayed image is the township plat used to locate the lots.","refs":"Archive: S-1670; S-1810; atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §3. GLO documents 1718 (57.24 acres) and 1717 (36.60 acres), 1 June 1858; subsequent entry, 10 May 1861. The displayed image is the township plat used to locate the lots.","image":"assets/plat-1851.jpg","body":"John’s 1858 federal land grants covered 57.24 acres in section 17 and 36.60 acres in section 18: 93.84 acres altogether. A further entry added 22.28 acres in section 17 in 1861.\n\nThe green outlines show the lots in those records, reconstructed from the 1851 township map. They are separate pieces of land. Keeping them visible later in the story does not mean John owned all of them continuously.","html":"<p>John’s 1858 federal land grants covered 57.24 acres in section 17 and 36.60 acres in section 18: 93.84 acres altogether. A further entry added 22.28 acres in section 17 in 1861.<\/p>\n<p>The green outlines show the lots in those records, reconstructed from the 1851 township map. They are separate pieces of land. Keeping them visible later in the story does not mean John owned all of them continuously.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/plat-1851.jpg","dimensions":[1800,1801],"displayBytes":435663,"sourceSha256":"19552f2b549148c20629907c1b38d1d7aea15ea13b0ecbd157d00a01747f3372"},"maryObit":{"id":"maryObit","title":"“Mrs. Lou Alford”","citation":"The Daily News, Bogalusa · 5 February 1946, page 4","refs":"S-1803; S-1814; C-208","image":"assets/mary-lou-obituary-1946.png","url":null,"caption":"Mary Lou’s death notice · Bogalusa Daily News · 5 February 1946 · excerpt","body":"The notice reports Lou Fussell Alford’s death at home near Mount Hermon on Monday morning, 4 February 1946. It names her husband Esco and daughter “Mrs. Christine Knight,” and announces services at Bogue Chitto church.\n\nIt describes Christine as living in Mount Hermon. That does not tell us whether she was there when her mother died.\n\nThe displayed image combines portions of the newspaper column. Read the opening at upper right, then the continuation on the left.","html":"<p>The notice reports Lou Fussell Alford’s death at home near Mount Hermon on Monday morning, 4 February 1946. It names her husband Esco and daughter “Mrs. Christine Knight,” and announces services at Bogue Chitto church.<\/p>\n<p>It describes Christine as living in Mount Hermon. That does not tell us whether she was there when her mother died.<\/p>\n<p>The displayed image combines portions of the newspaper column. Read the opening at upper right, then the continuation on the left.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/mary-lou-obituary-1946.jpg","dimensions":[1450,770],"displayBytes":130869,"sourceSha256":"e9f1d2c8bf1f5f18e62843e2c51ad8b8b94915d5cb5e009c8baecb5ff0c08e16"},"farm":{"id":"farm","title":"1850 agricultural return","citation":"Washington Parish · Schedule 4 · frame 836, row 25","refs":"S-1756","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The 1850 agricultural return lists forty improved acres, six hundred unimproved acres, four horses, 250 bushels of Indian corn and fifty bushels of oats. It values the farm at $300 and its implements at $100. “Improved” and “unimproved” are the census’s land categories.\n\nThe row is associated with this John Alford by comparison with the population census. It does not identify the farm’s location, prove ownership or show that it was Jacob’s earlier claim.","html":"<p>The 1850 agricultural return lists forty improved acres, six hundred unimproved acres, four horses, 250 bushels of Indian corn and fifty bushels of oats. It values the farm at $300 and its implements at $100. “Improved” and “unimproved” are the census’s land categories.<\/p>\n<p>The row is associated with this John Alford by comparison with the population census. It does not identify the farm’s location, prove ownership or show that it was Jacob’s earlier claim.<\/p>"},"census1880":{"id":"census1880","title":"John S. Alford in the 1880 census","citation":"Washington Parish · 1880 federal census · line 31","refs":"S-1613; C-140","image":null,"url":null,"body":"John S. Alford’s entry gives his own birthplace as Louisiana and both parents’ birthplaces as North Carolina. His twin brother Seaborn J.’s entry gives Virginia for both parents.\n\nThese are conflicting later reports of where their parents were born. Neither entry names the parents or identifies who supplied the information. They do not settle whether the twins’ father was Julius Alford’s son.","html":"<p>John S. Alford’s entry gives his own birthplace as Louisiana and both parents’ birthplaces as North Carolina. His twin brother Seaborn J.’s entry gives Virginia for both parents.<\/p>\n<p>These are conflicting later reports of where their parents were born. Neither entry names the parents or identifies who supplied the information. They do not settle whether the twins’ father was Julius Alford’s son.<\/p>"},"homestead":{"id":"homestead","title":"Seaborn’s homestead on the township plat","citation":"Archive: S-1670; atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §2; alford-direct-line-land-tenure-2026-09-08 patent reading. Patent signed 10 February 1881. The displayed plat supplies the lot positions; it predates the patent by thirty years.","refs":"Archive: S-1670; atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §2; alford-direct-line-land-tenure-2026-09-08 patent reading. Patent signed 10 February 1881. The displayed plat supplies the lot positions; it predates the patent by thirty years.","image":"assets/plat-1851.jpg","body":"Seaborn’s federal land grant was signed on 10 February 1881. It names lots 1, 2 and 3 of section 20, Township 1 South, Range 10 East. Their combined area was 135.24 acres.\n\nThe image shown is an earlier township map, used to locate those lots. The modern outline is reconstructed from the old map and present-day section boundaries. Neither Seaborn’s house nor John’s has been located.","html":"<p>Seaborn’s federal land grant was signed on 10 February 1881. It names lots 1, 2 and 3 of section 20, Township 1 South, Range 10 East. Their combined area was 135.24 acres.<\/p>\n<p>The image shown is an earlier township map, used to locate those lots. The modern outline is reconstructed from the old map and present-day section boundaries. Neither Seaborn’s house nor John’s has been located.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/plat-1851.jpg","dimensions":[1800,1801],"displayBytes":435663,"sourceSha256":"19552f2b549148c20629907c1b38d1d7aea15ea13b0ecbd157d00a01747f3372"},"candacyObit":{"id":"candacyObit","title":"Candacy Alford’s 1955 obituary","citation":"The Era-Leader, Thursday 24 November 1955, page 1, column 6, death notice for Candacy Bankston Alford.","refs":"S-1652; C-210","image":null,"body":"Candacy’s 1955 death notice names her son Jacob Alford and daughters Frankie Penny and Agnes Haley. It also names surviving stepchildren. Seaborn’s 1919 obituary reports three children from his marriage to Candacy; her later notice supplies their names.","html":"<p>Candacy’s 1955 death notice names her son Jacob Alford and daughters Frankie Penny and Agnes Haley. It also names surviving stepchildren. Seaborn’s 1919 obituary reports three children from his marriage to Candacy; her later notice supplies their names.<\/p>"}},"parts":{"1":"Early records","2":"John, Margaret, Seaborn & Laura","3":"Esco, Mary Lou & Christine","4":"Education, marriage & work","5":"Christine’s later life"}};
